.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt TRUNCATE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ftruncate ,
.Nm ftruncate64
.Nd change the length of an open file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn ftruncate "int fd" "off_t length"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn ftruncate
sets the length of the file referred to by
.Fa fd
to
.Fa length
bytes.
The descriptor must refer to a writable file on a mounted file system.
KRFFS, FAT32, exFAT, and ext2 support both shrinking and extending files.
An extended region reads as zeros.
A file attached to a loop device cannot be truncated, even to its
current length.
.Pp
After a successful call by a process whose effective uid is not 0,
the set-user-ID bit is cleared.
The set-group-ID bit is cleared if group execute permission is set.
.Pp
KRFFS and FAT32 accept lengths through 4294967295 bytes, subject to
available space.
The exFAT length limit is 9223372036854775807 bytes.
The ext2 limit depends on the volume's block size and is bounded by
its block pointers and 32-bit sector count.
The file system refuses a length beyond its limit.
.Ss System call interface
Musl's
.Vt off_t
is 64 bits on i386.
Its
.Fn ftruncate
uses system call 194,
.Nm ftruncate64 .
EBX is the descriptor; ECX and EDX are the low and high 32-bit halves
of the length.
With
.Dv _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE
defined, musl defines
.Nm ftruncate64
as a macro for
.Fn ftruncate ;
there is no separate prototype.
.Pp
The older system call 93,
.Nm ftruncate ,
takes the descriptor in EBX and an unsigned 32-bit length in ECX.
The 64-bit handler also treats the length as unsigned and leaves
the limit check to the file system.
A negative length passed through musl therefore exceeds the file
system's limit and yields
.Er EFBIG
when the descriptor and mount checks succeed.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn ftruncate
returns 0 on success, or -1 on failure with
.Va errno
set.
The kernel returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/vfsprobe.c
opens a three-byte file for reading and writing, truncates it to one
byte, and checks that a seek to the end returns 1.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOSPC]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is invalid, does not refer to a file, or has no mounted open-file
description.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The file backs an attached loop device.
.It Bq Er EFBIG
The requested length exceeds the file system's limit.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The descriptor is not writable, or ext2 was given a nonregular file.
.It Bq Er EIO
The device was removed, an input/output operation failed, or KRFFS
could not resolve or resize the file.
The KRFFS boot-root adapter also returns this error when there is
insufficient space to extend the file.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
exFAT could not find the containing directory while updating the file's
directory entries.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
exFAT could not allocate a node for the containing directory.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
A mounted KRFFS, FAT32, or exFAT volume has insufficient space to
extend the file.
.It Bq Er ENXIO
A FAT32 or exFAT cluster chain ends before a required cluster.
.It Bq Er EROFS
The file system is read-only or has no truncate operation.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
